{"result": "GLUTAMINA, or glutamine, is a non-essential amino acid that can become conditionally essential in certain stress situations, particularly during metabolic stress such as severe burns, post-surgical recovery, and in critically ill patients. It plays a crucial role in various metabolic processes, including nitrogen transport, immune response modulation, and serving as an energy source for enterocytes and intestinal cells. \n\nThis amino acid is often added to enteral diets to support gut health and is suggested to improve glycemic control in diabetic patients when supplemented in nutritional therapy. In the context of critical care, glutamine may be supplemented to manage nutritional needs, particularly in patients with burns or those undergoing chemotherapy and surgery, as it has been shown to help prevent nutritional problems and improve postoperative complication rates.\n\nResearch has indicated that glutamine can enhance intestinal barrier function, improve insulin sensitivity, and reduce hospital complications. It is also recognized for its potential benefits in patients with malabsorption, as it may aid in the absorption of water and electrolytes. However, the recommendations for its use in parenteral nutrition are not uniform across studies, and while it has been previously recommended for routine use, recent studies have raised questions about its efficacy.\n\nIn specific medical contexts, such as hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, glutamine has demonstrated beneficial effects, and it has been speculated to help alleviate symptoms of hepatic veno-occlusive disease in transplant patients. Additionally, glutamine is noted for its role as an immunomodulator, which can optimize recovery post-exercise and protect against mucositis during chemotherapy and radiotherapy treatments.\n\nDespite its potential benefits, the use of glutamine in clinical practice is sometimes questioned due to inconclusive evidence regarding its effectiveness, and it is no